
<% content_for :title, "Don't Poke the Bipolar Bear | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="bear-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="bear-hero">

    <picture class="bear-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_dont_poke_the_bipolar_bear_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_dont_poke_the_bipolar_bear_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="bear-hero-content">

      <p class="bear-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / BIPOLAR RAGE
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>DON'T POKE</span>
        <span>THE</span>
        <span>BIPOLAR BEAR.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="bear-intro">
        THE SHORT FUSE. THE FULL-BODY
        FORGET THIS. THE SPACE BEFORE YOU REACT.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / NOTICE THE BUILDUP -->
  <section class="bear-section bear-pink">
    <div class="bear-inner">

      <p class="bear-number">01 / NOTICE THE BUILDUP</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT'S<br>
        BUILDING UP?
      </h2>

      <div class="bear-copy">

        <p>
          Traffic won't move. Your computer
          freezes. You drop something when
          your back already hurts.
          One small inconvenience suddenly
          feels unbearable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then you get angry at yourself
          for getting so angry, and now
          you have two things to be
          pissed off about.
        </p>

        <p>
          Bipolar rage is a description
          of anger that feels consuming.
          Everything becomes one more
          thing testing an already
          very short fuse.
        </p>

        <p>
          Irritability can occur during
          mood episodes, but anger alone
          doesn't mean you're having one.
          Having bipolar disorder doesn't
          make you inherently aggressive.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pay attention to the intensity
          of your reaction and what else
          is happening with your mood.
          Recognizing the buildup gives
          you an opportunity to respond
          before things escalate.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="bear-grid">

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>IN YOUR BODY.</h3>

          <p>
            Your jaw clenches.
            Your hands tighten.
            Your muscles become tense,
            and you feel restless
            or physically agitated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything seems to
            demand an immediate reaction.
            Notice the physical tension
            before you act on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>IN YOUR THOUGHTS.</h3>

          <p>
            One inconvenience starts
            collecting every other
            problem you've been carrying.
          </p>

          <p>
            A lost document becomes
            "nothing ever works."
            An unfinished chore becomes
            "I can't manage my life."
          </p>

          <p>
            Recognize when you're adding
            judgments and predictions
            to the original problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>IN YOUR BEHAVIOR.</h3>

          <p>
            You become impatient,
            speak sharply, interrupt,
            or feel compelled to
            respond immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            You might slam something
            down, send an angry message,
            or continue arguing long
            after the conversation
            has stopped being useful.
          </p>

          <p>
            These are signs to pause,
            not instructions to
            keep going.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>AROUND YOU.</h3>

          <p>
            Noise, interruptions,
            delays, technology problems,
            physical pain, and unexpected
            demands can accumulate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Something relatively minor
            might become the final
            frustration in an already
            difficult day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Identify what's contributing
            to the buildup instead of
            treating everything as
            one enormous problem.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / MAKE SPACE -->
  <section class="bear-section bear-black">
    <div class="bear-inner">

      <p class="bear-number">02 / MAKE SPACE</p>

      <h2>
        STEP BACK<br>
        BEFORE YOU<br>
        SNAP.
      </h2>

      <div class="bear-copy">

        <p>
          When your body is highly
          activated, complicated
          reflection can wait.
          You don't need to analyze
          every thought while you're
          struggling not to react.
        </p>

        <p>
          Stop what you're doing.
          Set down the object.
          Stop typing. Pause the task.
          Step away from the conversation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Name what's happening:
          "I'm angry, and I want
          to react."
        </p>

        <p>
          You can recognize anger,
          tension, and the urge
          to act without treating
          that urge as an instruction.
        </p>

        <p>
          Give yourself time without
          continuing to rehearse
          the grievance. Replaying
          the argument and collecting
          more reasons to be angry
          isn't much of a break.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask yourself what response
          will leave you with fewer
          problems an hour from now.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes that means walking
          away from a conversation.
          Sometimes it means leaving
          a task alone for ten minutes.
          Sometimes it means deciding
          that the problem can wait.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're driving, prioritize
          safety over recovering lost time.
          Leave space, avoid reacting
          to other drivers, and pull
          over somewhere safe if
          you're too overwhelmed
          to continue driving.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="bear-callout">
        <p>
          "I'M GETTING TOO ANGRY TO
          HANDLE THIS WELL. I NEED TO
          STEP AWAY BEFORE THINGS GET
          GRIZZLY. WE CAN COME BACK
          TO THIS WHEN I'VE CALMED DOWN."
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / TURN IT DOWN -->
  <section class="bear-section bear-yellow">
    <div class="bear-inner">

      <p class="bear-number">03 / TURN IT DOWN</p>

      <h2>
        LESS INPUT.<br>
        MORE SPACE.
      </h2>

      <div class="bear-copy">

        <p>
          You can't always change
          the situation that's upsetting
          you. You can't force traffic
          to move, recover every lost
          document, or guarantee
          that your body will cooperate.
        </p>

        <p>
          But you can decide how
          much additional frustration
          you're going to feed
          into the situation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Give your attention
          something useful to do
          beyond listing how unfair
          everything is.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to enjoy
          the inconvenience or
          pretend it happened
          for a reason.
        </p>

        <p>
          Given that this happened,
          what response would help?
          A delay asks for patience.
          A broken plan asks for
          improvisation. A mistake
          asks for repair.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes success simply
          means leaving something
          alone long enough
          to settle down.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="bear-grid">

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>YOUR SURROUNDINGS.</h3>

          <p>
            Reduce unnecessary noise
            and stimulation.
            Find somewhere quiet
            where you're less likely
            to be interrupted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave an argument
            or frustrating task
            temporarily if you
            can do so safely.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need to
            solve the original
            problem immediately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>YOUR BODY.</h3>

          <p>
            Notice your breathing,
            clenched jaw, tight hands,
            and other physical signs
            of agitation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try releasing some of
            that tension and finding
            a comfortable position.
          </p>

          <p>
            If pain is contributing
            to your anger, follow
            your established
            pain-management plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't force physical
            activity that aggravates
            your symptoms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>YOUR PHONE.</h3>

          <p>
            Stop typing.
            Put your phone down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't send the angry
            message or continue
            an argument through text.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need to get
            your thoughts out,
            write them privately
            without sending them.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can decide what
            needs to be communicated
            when you're no longer
            reacting impulsively.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>YOUR NEXT FEW MINUTES.</h3>

          <p>
            Take a break from
            the problem itself.
            Listen to music,
            make tea, spend some
            time with Banjo,
            or choose another
            quiet activity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try not to use
            the entire break
            to continue the
            argument in your head.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check whether you're
            becoming less agitated
            before deciding what
            to do next.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / CHECK THE CONTEXT -->
  <section class="bear-section bear-purple">
    <div class="bear-inner">

      <p class="bear-number">04 / CHECK THE CONTEXT</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ELSE<br>
        IS GOING ON?
      </h2>

      <div class="bear-copy">

        <p>
          Once you've settled enough
          to think more clearly,
          consider what your anger
          is connected to.
        </p>

        <p>
          One problem can collect
          every other frustration
          you've been carrying.
          Pain interrupts your day,
          and suddenly you're
          remembering everything
          your body has kept
          you from doing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Come back to the specific
          event. What happened?
          What are you predicting?
          What judgment have
          you added?
        </p>

        <p>
          Losing your work and
          needing to redo it
          is frustrating enough
          without turning it
          into "I'm a failure."
        </p>

        <p>
          Understanding the context
          doesn't mean dismissing
          your anger. It helps
          you respond to the
          actual problem instead
          of every problem at once.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="bear-grid">

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>SLEEP &amp; MOOD CHANGES.</h3>

          <p>
            Have you been sleeping
            less than usual?
            Are your thoughts racing?
            Have you become unusually
            irritable or impulsive?
          </p>

          <p>
            Irritability can occur
            during manic and mixed
            episodes, but it isn't
            enough by itself
            to identify one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the broader
            pattern and contact
            your psych if your
            usual warning signs
            are appearing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>PAIN &amp; OVERLOAD.</h3>

          <p>
            Are you exhausted
            from chronic pain?
            Have you been struggling
            with your mobility,
            sleep, or responsibilities?
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes the anger
            is connected to
            helplessness, exhaustion,
            or frustration with
            what your body
            won't let you do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recognize those limitations
            and adjust what
            you're expecting
            of yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>THE SITUATION ITSELF.</h3>

          <p>
            What actually happened?
            What part of the situation
            needs to be addressed?
          </p>

          <p>
            Separate the original
            event from everything
            you've added to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider whether you're
            dealing with a genuine
            boundary violation,
            an inconvenience,
            a mistake, or
            a misunderstanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Different problems
            call for different
            responses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>SUPPORT YOU MIGHT NEED.</h3>

          <p>
            Sometimes you need
            someone to listen.
            Sometimes you need
            another perspective
            or help figuring out
            what to do next.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be careful with
            commiserating.
            Repeatedly replaying
            a grievance with
            someone who agrees
            with everything can
            make you both angrier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try asking someone
            to let you vent
            briefly, then help
            you consider your
            options.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're thinking
            about drinking to
            calm down, use
            your relapse plan
            and contact support.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / SAY WHAT YOU NEED -->
  <section class="bear-section bear-black">
    <div class="bear-inner">

      <p class="bear-number">05 / SAY WHAT YOU NEED</p>

      <h2>
        FIND THE WORDS.<br>
        LEAVE THE<br>
        DAMAGE.
      </h2>

      <div class="bear-copy">

        <p>
          You can be angry
          without making the
          situation worse.
        </p>

        <p>
          When you're ready
          to address the problem,
          focus on what actually
          happened and what
          needs to change.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to
          bring up every previous
          grievance or convince
          the other person that
          your anger is justified.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes the useful
          response is a conversation.
          Sometimes it's establishing
          a boundary, correcting
          a mistake, or accepting
          that an inconvenience
          can't be undone.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you've already
          said or done something
          hurtful, acknowledge it.
          Address what happened
          without turning your
          mistake into another
          reason to hate yourself.
        </p>

        <p>
          Accountability means
          recognizing the behavior,
          dealing with its consequences,
          and considering what
          would help next time.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="bear-grid">

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>ASK FOR SPACE.</h3>

          <p>
            "I'm getting really
            frustrated, and I
            need some time before
            we continue this
            conversation. I'd
            rather come back
            to it when I'm
            thinking more clearly."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>NAME THE PROBLEM.</h3>

          <p>
            "What happened earlier
            really bothered me.
            I'd like to explain
            why and figure out
            how we can handle
            it differently."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>SET A BOUNDARY.</h3>

          <p>
            "I'm willing to
            discuss this, but
            I don't want to
            keep arguing.
            If the conversation
            continues like this,
            I'm going to
            step away."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>RETURN TO THE CONVERSATION.</h3>

          <p>
            "I've had some time
            to think about what
            happened. I'm still
            upset, but I'd like
            to work through it
            without making
            another argument
            out of it."
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / YOUR PLAN -->
  <section class="bear-section bear-pink">
    <div class="bear-inner">

      <p class="bear-number">06 / YOUR PLAN</p>

      <h2>
        KNOW YOUR<br>
        COOLING-OFF<br>
        PLAN.
      </h2>

      <div class="bear-copy">

        <p>
          You won't handle every
          frustration gracefully.
          Sometimes you'll react
          before you've recognized
          how angry you are.
        </p>

        <p>
          When that happens,
          address what you can
          and consider what
          would help next time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you needed an
          earlier break, a
          practical limit,
          or another person's
          support.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can explore what
          your anger is protecting:
          helplessness, fear of
          losing control, exhaustion
          from pain, or frustration
          with your circumstances.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to force
          an explanation. You can
          recognize the anger
          while still paying attention
          to the parts of you
          that want stability,
          safety, and sobriety.
        </p>

        <p>
          Notice the reckless
          decision you don't make,
          the message you don't
          send, and the problem
          you address without
          spending the rest of
          the day criticizing yourself.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="bear-grid">

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>MY EARLY WARNING SIGNS.</h3>

          <p>
            A clenched jaw,
            tight hands,
            increasing tension,
            and disappearing patience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Racing thoughts,
            replaying grievances,
            and feeling that
            everything requires
            an immediate reaction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Snapping, speaking
            sharply, or becoming
            disproportionately upset
            by small inconveniences.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>WHAT HELPS ME COOL DOWN.</h3>

          <p>
            Put down whatever
            is frustrating you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stop typing and
            step away from
            the conversation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reduce stimulation
            and allow time
            for physical tension
            to settle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Find a comfortable
            position for your back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Focus on what you
            can reasonably do
            about the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid spending the
            entire break replaying
            the grievance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>WHO I'LL CONTACT.</h3>

          <p>
            A trusted person
            who can listen
            without escalating
            the situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your therapist if
            anger is becoming
            difficult to manage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your psychiatrist
            if irritability occurs
            alongside changes
            in sleep, energy,
            thoughts, or behavior.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recovery support if
            anger is making
            drinking sound appealing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bear-box">
          <h3>WHEN I NEED URGENT SUPPORT.</h3>

          <p>
            If you're afraid
            you might hurt yourself
            or someone else,
            step away and get
            immediate support.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't drive while
            you're too agitated
            to do so safely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seek urgent psychiatric
            assessment if agitation
            is escalating alongside
            severe insomnia, paranoia,
            psychotic symptoms,
            or dangerous impulsivity.
          </p>

          <p>
            If there's immediate
            danger, contact
            emergency services.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="bear-callout">
        <p>
          THE PAUSE COUNTS.
          SO DOES THE RECKLESS MOVE
          YOU DON'T MAKE, THE OBJECT
          YOU PUT DOWN, AND THE
          MISTAKE YOU ADDRESS
          WITHOUT HATING YOURSELF.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
